    "NPORT_release_anchor": [
      {
        "id": "P02",
        "prompt": "Answer the question briefly and explain your answer. Do not use web search or external tools.\n\nAs of May 1, 2025, what is the scheduled compliance date for the new monthly Form N-PORT regime for fund groups with at least $1 billion in net assets?",
        "reference": {
          "date": "2027-11-17"
        },
        "response": "ChatGPT said:\n\nAs of May 1, 2025, the scheduled compliance date was November 17, 2026, for fund groups with at least $1 billion in net assets.\n\nThe SEC extended the original November 17, 2025 deadline by one year, giving larger fund groups additional time to implement the new monthly Form N-PORT reporting requirements.",
        "sha256": "b7c048e3984435abed3c284a826b671246923989379da1e8cad6fbe1173083f0"
      },
      {
        "id": "P41",
        "prompt": "Answer the question briefly and explain your answer. Do not use web search or external tools.\n\nAs of May 26, 2026, when does the portfolio holdings information in a Form N-PORT report for the third month of a registered fund's fiscal quarter become publicly available?",
        "reference": {
          "release": "upon filing, subject to confidential-field exceptions"
        },
        "response": "ChatGPT said:\n\nAs of May 26, 2026, portfolio holdings information in a Form N-PORT report for the third month of a registered fund\u2019s fiscal quarter becomes publicly available 60 days after the end of that fiscal quarter.\n\nExplanation: Under the applicable SEC reporting framework, Form N-PORT reports are submitted to the SEC, but only the report for the third month of each fiscal quarter is routinely made public. This 60-day delay allows funds time to report their holdings before the information becomes publicly accessible.",
        "sha256": "042d5c38da1b62bb3cc28915a404431694f61df96f3502b15b823c9608717357"
      },
      {
        "id": "P42",
        "prompt": "Answer the question briefly and explain your answer. Do not use web search or external tools.\n\nA registered fund has a fiscal quarter ending March 31, 2026. Its Form N-PORT for March 31, 2026 was filed and accepted by EDGAR at 10:00 a.m. Eastern Time on May 20, 2026. The submission was processed normally. Can an analyst performing a public-source portfolio analysis at noon Eastern Time on May 26, 2026 use the report's ordinary portfolio holdings information? Explain the publication timing.",
        "reference": {
          "usable": true,
          "release": "upon filing on 2026-05-20"
        },
        "response": "ChatGPT said:\n\nNo. At noon Eastern Time on May 26, 2026, the analyst cannot use the report\u2019s ordinary portfolio holdings information as publicly available Form N-PORT data.\n\nUnder the Form N-PORT publication rules, ordinary portfolio holdings information for the third month of a fiscal quarter becomes public 60 days after the quarter-end, not immediately upon EDGAR filing and acceptance.\n\nQuarter-end: March 31, 2026\n\nEDGAR acceptance: May 20, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. ET\n\n60-day publication date: May 30, 2026\n\nAnalysis date: May 26, 2026, at noon ET\n\nBecause May 26 falls before the 60-day publication date, the holdings information is not yet public through the normal Form N-PORT dissemination process.\n\nKey distinction: EDGAR acceptance establishes that the filing was received and processed; it does not override the delayed-publication rule. Since May 30 is a Saturday, actual EDGAR public availability may occur on the next business day, Monday, June 1, 2026.",
        "sha256": "2ec3c69b9168ca78cfc2a6711eecef2a0f2799098f8578efb03287a192815aec"
      },
      {
        "id": "P43",
        "prompt": "Answer the question briefly and explain your answer. Do not use web search or external tools.\n\nA registered fund has a fiscal quarter ending March 31, 2026. Its Form N-PORT for March 31, 2026 was filed and accepted by EDGAR at 10:00 a.m. Eastern Time on May 20, 2026. The submission was processed normally. Can an analyst performing a public-source portfolio analysis at noon Eastern Time on May 26, 2026 use the report's ordinary portfolio holdings information? Explain the publication timing.\n\nSEC Release IC-33384, page 8, states: \"The reports on Form N-PORT for the first two months of the quarterly filing period will continue to be non-public and the quarter-end report (i.e., the last month of the quarter) will become publicly available immediately upon filing, with the exception of the items identified in General Instruction F of Form N-PORT, which will continue to remain non-public.\"\nSource: https://www.sec.gov/files/rules/interim/2019/ic-33384.pdf",
        "reference": {
          "usable": true,
          "release": "upon filing on 2026-05-20"
        },
        "response": "ChatGPT said:\n\nYes. The analyst can use the report\u2019s ordinary portfolio holdings information at noon Eastern Time on May 26, 2026.\n\nUnder SEC Release IC-33384, page 8, the quarter-end Form N-PORT report becomes publicly available immediately upon filing, except for information identified in General Instruction F, which remains non-public.\n\nBecause the March 31, 2026 report was filed, accepted, and processed normally at 10:00 a.m. ET on May 20, 2026, its ordinary portfolio holdings information became publicly available at that time.\n\nThus, by noon ET on May 26, 2026, the information had been public for more than six days. No additional publication waiting period applies to ordinary quarter-end holdings information.",
        "sha256": "e475d58427ea9cbd3febb6dc12fa833c6d6d8f786eebf6d196b3d5270c8ef147"
      },
      {
        "id": "P44",
        "prompt": "Answer the question briefly and explain your answer. Do not use web search or external tools.\n\nAs of May 26, 2026, what does the 60-day timing requirement for quarterly Form N-PORT submissions govern: the submission deadline, the public-release date, or both? Explain how it relates to a third-month report filed before that limit.",
        "reference": {
          "sixty_days": "filing deadline, not mandatory public-release embargo",
          "early_report_release": "upon filing subject to exceptions"
        },
        "response": "ChatGPT said:\n\nUnder the quarterly Form N-PORT filing regime, the 60-day requirement governs the submission deadline, not a mandatory public-release date.\n\nFunds must submit their quarterly Form N-PORT reports within 60 days after the fiscal quarter ends. Generally, the third-month report becomes publicly available when filed, while reports for the first two months remain nonpublic.\n\nTherefore, if a fund files its third-month report on day 45, it can become publicly available on day 45. It does not have to wait until day 60.\n\nBottom line: The 60-day rule establishes the latest filing date, not a required 60-day delay before public disclosure.",
        "sha256": "d0f62742f10b90a6c3c1ddf2627588294a6e432808e4461c83bf3f7fd21edb37"
      }
    ]
  },
  "opening_case": {
